Under classical bivalence, the statement $P$ has two possible truth
values. If $P$ is true, its negation $\htmlData{mlg-ref=5c6e6f74}{
eg P}$ is false, and the
negation of that result $\htmlData{mlg-ref=5c6e6f74}{
eg \left(\htmlData{mlg-ref=5c6e6f74}{
eg P}ight)}$ is true. Conversely, if
$P$ is false, $\htmlData{mlg-ref=5c6e6f74}{
eg P}$ is true, and $\htmlData{mlg-ref=5c6e6f74}{
eg \left(\htmlData{mlg-ref=5c6e6f74}{
eg P}ight)}$ is false.
In both cases, $\htmlData{mlg-ref=5c6e6f74}{
eg \left(\htmlData{mlg-ref=5c6e6f74}{
eg P}ight)}$ has the identical truth value as
$P$. Therefore, $\htmlData{mlg-ref=5c2e6966662e2f}{\htmlData{mlg-ref=5c6e6f74}{
eg \left(\htmlData{mlg-ref=5c6e6f74}{
eg P}ight)} \iff P}$.
